  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights plotted shows a trough moving \nthrough the Great Lakes area. A secondary trailing shortwave was \nevident moving through the IA/IL area. At the surface, a cold front \nmoved through earlier, shifting winds to the north and allowing \ncooler/drier dewpoints to build south into the area as high pressure \nmoves into the Northern and Central Plains. This high pressure will \nbe the main feature influencing our weather through the end of \nthe week. With that high moving through the region Thursday, \nhighs look to be in the low to mid 80s across the area. But \ndewpoints settling into the lower 60s will be noticeably drier.\nThat high pressure area shifts east by Friday, starting a \nwarming trend as low-level flow trends southerly again. This \nshould lead to highs a few degrees warmer than Thursday. \nMoisture will lag the increase in temperatures so we'll still \nsee lower humidity levels Friday. \n\nFor the weekend and into next week, the upper ridge that was \ncentered over the western half of the US will shift east, aiding the \nwarmup, and persistent southerly flow will allow for greater \nmoisture to return into the area. High temperatures will climb \nback into the 90s with this pattern with dewpoints climbing back\ninto the middle to upper 60s Saturday, and potentially into the\nlower 70s by Sunday. For now, Monday looks like the hottest day\nof this forecast with highs climbing into the middle and upper \n90s. The GFS is a strong warm outlier here and looking at a violin\nplot of max temperatures in the forecast area for Monday, it \nclearly shows the GFS is a very warm outlier and well above the \n90th percentile of guidance. Our forecast is closer to the 75th \npercentile. With the increasing dewpoints factored in, our heat \nindex values climb back into the 100-105 range for most \nlocations Sunday and Monday, with the brunt of the heat focused \nsouth of the Missouri river Tuesday and Wednesday. We'll need to\nmonitor this period for possible heat headlines as a small \nincrease in moisture will lead to more widespread heat index \nvalues near 105. In fact, by early next week, there is a greater\nthan 75% chance for seeing at least major HeatRisk and low \nprobabilities of extreme HeatRisk.\n\nRegarding precipitation chances through the forecast, they look\nsmall and mainly across far northern Missouri, along the periphery\nof the upper ridge for next week.",
